{"doc_id": "doc_361233315d24", "identity": "A full draft federal constitution of the Republic of Yemen (mudawwanat dustur jumhuriyyat al-Yaman al-Ittihadiyya), rendered as running text with punctuation stripped and original line breaks preserved as <NL>. Formal legislative Arabic: books (bab), chapters (fasl), unnumbered articles, and heavy use of enumerated sub-provisions. 16000 tokens, 529 paragraph units. Registers as normative prose: verb-initial provisions (taltazim / yuhzar / yunazzim / yatawalla ...) and nominal rights formulas (li-kull ... al-haqq fi ...). Ends with the closing formula wa-Allahu al-muwaffiq.", "law": "The sentence unit is ONE normative text unit: an article, a sub-provision of an article, an enumerated list item, or a heading. Most paragraphs are exactly one unit, so every token before <NL> is a boundary (given). Internal boundaries exist only where the source line break was lost during transcription, and they fall at exactly three site types. (1) ABSORBED HEADING: a section/organ title (al-Bab al-Thani, hay-at Makafahat al-Fasad, al-Bi-a, insha- al-sanadiq, thaniyan al-sulta al-tanfidhiyya) got glued to the end of the preceding article; the boundary is on the last token of the article, immediately before the title. (2) ASYNDETIC NEW PROVISION: two articles or numbered sub-clauses merged into one line; the second starts a fresh predication with NO clitic conjunction. Arabic orthography makes this decidable, since wa- and fa- are prefixed to the following word: a clause-initial token WITHOUT wa-/fa- and not introduced by aw / thumma / kama / bal / lakin is asyndetic. (3) ENUMERATION ITEM: an inline list under a colon-stem (al-atiya / bi-al-ati / ma yali / al-shurut al-atiya); each item ends a unit. THREE COUNTER-RULES suppress a boundary. (a) The wa-/fa- rule: material beginning with a clitic conjunction continues the same sentence, however long the chain (this is why 90-token articles like the arrest provision stay whole). (b) The attributive-verb rule: a bare imperfect verb whose subject IS the immediately preceding noun is a relative/descriptive clause, not a new sentence - hay-a wataniyya mustaqilla tatawalla..., ma-a wa-sittun udwan yatimmu intikhabuhum..., mu-amala tadmanu himayatahum. (c) The apodosis rule: after idha / fa-idha / in / ma lam / matta the following verb is the answer clause and belongs to the same sentence. FOURTH: no boundary after a colon-stem - the stem is grammatically incomplete and a colon is not sentence-final, so the introducer attaches to the first list item.", "reasoning": "What the enumerations are was the decisive question, since ~300 candidate sites hang on it. P401-P406 settles it: the Constitutional Court competence list is split by the source itself - the stem plus item 1 sit in one paragraph, items 2-5 are four separate <NL> paragraphs, and items 6-10 run inline inside one paragraph. Since every token before <NL> is a guaranteed boundary, items 2-5 are gold sentences; the inline items of the same single list must therefore be sentences too, or the gold contradicts itself within one article. P9-P11 corroborates: the list under yaqumu al-nizam al-siyasi ala asas is rendered as separate paragraphs there, while the parallel list under yaqumu ala al-usus al-atiya (P19) is inline. So the source lost line breaks unevenly; the period after each item survived into the gold and was then stripped. Same mechanism explains absorbed headings, which is my highest-confidence class - al-nizam al-siyasi at 169, al-Bab al-Thani al-huquq wa-al-hurriyyat at 1898, hay-at al-khidma al-madaniyya at 9939, insha- al-sanadiq at 15914 are titles for the NEXT article, welded onto the previous line; the article before them ends and takes a boundary. I did NOT split at the colon-stem: hay-at huquq al-insan ... wa-dhalika min khilal ma yali (10024) is plainly incomplete without its first item, so the stem rides with item 1 and the first boundary is at al-dawla (10046). I rejected many superficially attractive verb-initial sites using the attributive test - al-sulta al-qada-iyya mustaqilla tatawalla iqamat al-adl (7372) keeps its subject, so no split, whereas al-shabab quwwa fa-ila fi bina- al-watan | takfulu al-DAWLA ri-ayatahum (1483) switches subject and does split. The apodosis test killed fa-in lam tatimm al-da-wa | yajtami al-majlisan (4853), idha aqarra ... duna ta-dil | yatimmu raf mashru al-qanun (5718) and ma lam tashtarit aghlabiyya khassa | tuttakhadh al-qararat (4969). Two sites I flag as genuinely uncertain and mark lo: 11868 (al-ikhtisasat al-hasriyya li-al-wilaya wa-al-mahalliyyat reads as a stranded title rather than a colon-stem, so I treated it as an absorbed heading) and 15997 (wa-Allahu al-muwaffiq begins with wa-, which my own rule forbids splitting before, but it is a fixed closing doxology that cannot be coordinated with a normative provision). One late catch: P337 and P344, the Sanaa and Aden city-council articles, are verbatim twins each packing three provisions - ...wifqan li-nizam al-qa-ima al-nisbiyya | muddat al-majlis arba sanawat ... wa-yunazzam bi-qanun | yushtarat fi al-mutarashshih... - giving 9244/9257 and 9439/9452; I found them only by sweeping for spans over 60 tokens with no boundary, and every other such span turned out to be a legitimate single wa-chained sentence. Total 991 boundaries: 529 paragraph-final (given) plus 462 internal.", "boundaries": [{"i": 3, "w": "العامة", "c": "hi"}, {"i": 8, "w": "السياسية", "c": "hi"}, {"i": 46, "w": "والإسلامية", "c": "hi"}, {"i": 54, "w": "الرسمية", "c": "hi"}, {"i": 61, "w": "والسقطرية", "c": "hi"}, {"i": 75, "w": "التشريعية", "c": "hi"}, {"i": 97, "w": "والقضائية", "c": "hi"}, {"i": 131, "w": "اليمن", "c": "hi"}, {"i": 156, "w": "سحبها", "c": "hi"}, {"i": 167, "w": "واحده", "c": "hi"}, {"i": 169, "w": "السياسي", "c": "hi"}, {"i": 175, "w": "أساس", "c": "hi"}, {"i": 208, "w": "العسكرية", "c": "hi"}, {"i": 219, "w": "والتداولية", "c": "hi"}, {"i": 243, "w": "العنف", "c": "hi"}, 